NVIDIA Corporation is an American technology company that pioneered GPU-accelerated computing and is the leading force behind the artificial intelligence (AI) revolution. The company's business model has shifted from a pure focus on gaming graphics to providing full-stack computing infrastructure that powers data centers, autonomous vehicles, and industrial digitalization. 
What Nvidia Does
Nvidia designs and sells graphics processing units (GPUs), systems on a chip (SoCs), and related software and services for high-performance computing, AI, gaming, professional visualization, and automotive markets. The company's key innovation is the CUDA software platform, which unlocked the parallel processing power of GPUs for a wide range of computationally intensive applications beyond traditional graphics rendering. 
Its operations are divided into two primary segments: 
Compute & Networking: This is now Nvidia's largest and fastest-growing revenue segment, focusing on data center accelerated computing platforms, AI solutions, networking, and autonomous vehicle technology.
Graphics: This segment focuses on GPUs for gaming PCs, professional visualization workstations, and cloud-based graphics rendering. 
Products and Services Sold
Nvidia's offerings span both hardware and a robust software ecosystem, creating a comprehensive platform for developers and consumers alike. 
Hardware Products
GeForce GPUs: Graphics cards for gaming PCs and laptops.
NVIDIA RTX/Quadro GPUs: Professional-grade graphics cards for enterprise workstations and creative workloads.
Data Center Platforms (HGX, DGX, MGX): Integrated hardware systems and supercomputers (e.g., the Grace Blackwell architecture) designed for AI training and deployment.
Networking Hardware: High-speed Ethernet and InfiniBand interconnect solutions, including Data Processing Units (DPUs) and SuperNICs.
Embedded Systems: The Jetson platform for robotics and edge AI applications, and the DRIVE platform for in-vehicle computing for autonomous vehicles. 
Software and Services
CUDA: Nvidia's foundational parallel computing platform and programming model, which has a vast developer ecosystem and is crucial for AI model development.
NVIDIA AI Enterprise & NIM: A suite of AI software, tools, and microservices for the secure and reliable deployment of high-performance AI models across clouds and data centers.
GeForce NOW: A cloud gaming service that allows users to stream high-end games to various devices.
NVIDIA Omniverse: A platform for building and operating industrial AI and digital twin applications for use in design, simulation, and robotics.
NVIDIA Studio: A platform providing high-performance laptops and desktops, with dedicated software and drivers for creators and designers. 
Corporate Governance and Board Members
Nvidia is a public company (NASDAQ: NVDA) with a diverse ownership structure, where institutional investors hold the majority of shares. 
Executive Leadership
Jensen Huang: Founder, President, and Chief Executive Officer (CEO)
Colette Kress: Executive Vice President and Chief Financial Officer (CFO)
Debora Shoquist: Executive Vice President, Operations
Tim Teter: Senior Vice President, General Counsel and Secretary 
